      <span class="page-breadcrumb-sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/blog">Blog</a>
      <span class="page-breadcrumb-sep" aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span aria-current="page">How to Make a T-Shirt Mockup for Free</span>
    </nav>
  </div>

  <div class="page-content-wide">
    <article class="prose" style="max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto;">
      <h1>How to make a t-shirt mockup for free (no Photoshop needed)</h1>
      <p><time datetime="2026-07-12">July 12, 2026</time></p>

      <p>
        A mockup is the difference between selling a shirt and selling an idea of a shirt. Buyers on
        Etsy, Shopify, and print-on-demand marketplaces almost never see your actual product before
        it ships — they see a photo of your design on a garment. If that photo looks flat or fake,
        the design gets skipped no matter how good it is.
      </p>
      <p>
        The good news: you don't need Photoshop, a photographer, or a paid template. This guide
        walks through making a professional t-shirt mockup in your browser, for free, in about a
        minute — plus the placement and sizing details that make a mockup look real instead of
        pasted on.
      </p>

      <p>
        <img
          src="/assets/banner/blog_howto_mockup.jpg"
          width="1200"
          height="630"
          loading="eager"
          fetchpriority="high"
          alt="A transparent PNG design file being turned into a finished t-shirt mockup on a navy tee, on a white wood background"
          style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 12px;"
        />
      </p>

      <h2>What you need before you start</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your design as a PNG with a transparent background.</strong> This matters more
          than anything else. With transparency, the shirt fabric shows through around your
          artwork; without it, you get a white rectangle behind everything. Most design tools
          (Canva, Figma, Illustrator, Procreate) export transparent PNGs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A browser.</strong> That's it. No account, no install, no credit card.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Step 1 — Pick your garment</h2>
      <p>
        Open the <a href="/editor">free mockup editor</a> and choose the garment that matches what
        you're actually selling. TeeMockup has eight:
        <a href="/crewneck-mockup-generator">crewneck tee</a>,
        <a href="/v-neck-mockup-generator">v-neck</a>,
        <a href="/ladies-tee-mockup-generator">ladies tee</a>,
        <a href="/long-sleeve-mockup-generator">long sleeve</a>,
        <a href="/polo-shirt-mockup-generator">polo</a>,
        <a href="/tank-top-mockup-generator">tank top</a>,
        <a href="/sweatshirt-mockup-generator">sweatshirt</a>, and
        <a href="/hoodie-mockup-generator">hoodie</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Matching the mockup to the real product sounds obvious, but it's a common mistake: showing
        a unisex crewneck mockup for a listing that ships a ladies' fitted tee leads to returns and
        bad reviews. If you sell one design on several garments, make a mockup for each — it takes
        seconds and multi-garment listings convert better.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 2 — Upload your design</h2>
      <p>
        Drop your PNG into the editor. Your file stays in your browser — nothing is uploaded to a
        server, which also means there's no processing wait and no privacy question about
        unreleased designs.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 3 — Position and size it like a real print</h2>
      <p>
        This is where most DIY mockups give themselves away. Drag your design into place and keep
        three rules in mind:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Width: roughly 60–70% of the chest.</strong> A full-width print looks like a
          costume; a tiny centered logo looks like a pocket print (which is fine, if that's the
          intent). Standard print-on-demand print areas are around 12″ wide on a 20″-wide shirt.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Vertical position: the top of the design about 2–3″ below the collar.</strong>
          Designs centered on the belly are the most common beginner tell.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Respect the fabric.</strong> The editor renders 3D fabric folds over your
          artwork, which is what makes the print read as <em>on</em> the shirt rather than floating
          in front of it. If your design looks perfectly flat and crisp while the shirt has folds,
          buyers notice something is off even if they can't say what.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Want the exact numbers for every placement — left chest, back prints, hoodies, fitted tees?
        See the full <a href="/blog/t-shirt-design-placement-size-guide">design placement &amp; size guide</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 4 — Download your PNG</h2>
      <p>
        Hit download and you get a 1000×1000 pixel PNG — no watermark, cleared for commercial use.
        That resolution displays cleanly on every major marketplace: Etsy thumbnails, Shopify
        product grids, Amazon Merch, Redbubble, all of them.
      </p>

      <h2>Tips that separate good mockups from great ones</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Make more than one.</strong> Your first image should be the clean product shot;
          follow-ups can show the design on a <a href="/hoodie-mockup-generator">hoodie</a> or
          <a href="/sweatshirt-mockup-generator">sweatshirt</a> to upsell other garments.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Check contrast against the garment color.</strong> A black-line design on a dark
          shirt disappears in thumbnails. If your design has thin dark strokes, preview it small —
          marketplace search results render your mockup at under 300px.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep your source design high-resolution.</strong> Upload the same 4500×5400px
          file you'd send to your print provider. The editor scales it down cleanly; a low-res
          upload scaled up gets blurry.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reuse the mockup everywhere.</strong> The same PNG works for your listing, social
          posts, and ads — consistent product imagery across channels measurably lifts trust.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Selling on a marketplace? Go deeper</h2>
      <p>
        Mockup requirements and buyer expectations differ by platform. We've written specific
        guides for <a href="/t-shirt-mockups-for-etsy">Etsy sellers</a>,
        <a href="/t-shirt-mockups-for-shopify">Shopify stores</a>, and
        <a href="/print-on-demand-mockups">print-on-demand sellers</a> in general.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>Do I need Photoshop to make a t-shirt mockup?</h3>
      <p>
        No. A browser-based mockup generator places your design on a garment photo automatically —
        no design software, templates, or smart objects needed.
      </p>
      <h3>What file format should my design be?</h3>
      <p>
        A PNG with a transparent background works best, because anything behind your artwork shows
        the shirt fabric instead of a white box. JPG works too if your design fills a solid shape.
      </p>
      <h3>Can I use free mockups for Etsy or Shopify listings?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes — mockups from TeeMockup are free for commercial use with no watermark, so you can use
        them directly as listing images on Etsy, Shopify, or any print-on-demand marketplace.
      </p>
      <h3>What size should a t-shirt mockup image be?</h3>
      <p>
        1000×1000 pixels or larger covers most marketplaces. Etsy recommends at least 2000px on the
        shortest side for zoom, but a crisp 1000×1000 PNG displays cleanly in search results and
        listings.
      </p>
    </article>

    <div class="about-cta-block">
      <h2>Make your first mockup now</h2>
      <p>Under 60 seconds from design file to finished mockup — free, no signup, no watermark.</p>
      <a href="/editor" class="btn-hero-primary">Open the Free T-Shirt Mockup Generator</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</PageLayout>
